<section class="section" aria-labelledby="nfc-three-steps">
  <p class="eyebrow">ONE CARD. THREE SIMPLE STEPS.</p>

  <h2 id="nfc-three-steps">
    More than a business card.
    <br />
    <em>A conversation starter.</em>
  </h2>

  <p>
    A business card should do more than share your name and number. It should
    introduce your brand, spark curiosity, and give people a reason to continue
    the conversation. NFC business cards connect a physical introduction with
    information people can explore, keep, and act on.
  </p>

  <p>
    NFC stands for <strong>Near Field Communication</strong>. A small chip inside
    the card communicates with a compatible smartphone over a very short
    distance. Depending on its setup, the card can share supported information
    directly or point someone towards an online destination.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison">
    <article>
      <h4>STEP 01</h4>
      <h3>Tap to make the connection.</h3>

      <p>
        Hold your card near the NFC reader on a compatible smartphone. The
        recipient may need to unlock their phone or enable NFC first. What
        happens next depends on the phone, its software, and the type of
        information stored on the chip.
      </p>

      <p>
        A supported website link typically produces a notification inviting
        someone to open it. Contact information stored directly on the chip
        requires a reader that supports that record format, so the experience
        is not identical across every device.
      </p>

      <p>
        A printed QR code offers another way to share information when tapping
        is unavailable. The NFC interaction itself does not need internet, although opening an online destination does.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h4>STEP 02</h4>
      <h3>Share more than your details.</h3>

      <p>
        Start with what matters: your name, business, and contact information.
        Then consider what would help someone understand your value. A card
        configured with a website link can connect people to a richer online
        experience.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A portfolio that showcases your latest work.</li>
        <li>Service information or a product catalogue.</li>
        <li>Social profiles where people can follow your business.</li>
        <li>A booking page or an enquiry form.</li>
        <li>Videos, demonstrations, or customer testimonials.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        These online capabilities come from the linked destination, not from
        the chip alone. Direct-data cards store supported information on the
        card itself, with available space and reader compatibility determining
        what can be shared.
      </p>

      <p>
        Updating direct data means rewriting a writable chip. A webpage you
        control can be updated without changing the card, provided its stored
        web address stays the same.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article>
      <h4>STEP 03</h4>
      <h3>Turn curiosity into conversation.</h3>

      <p>
        The real opportunity is what happens after the introduction. Instead
        of simply saying, “Here are my details,” give someone a reason to
        explore: <strong>“Let me show you what we can create.”</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        A designer could share a portfolio link. A consultant could introduce
        a useful resource. A photographer could showcase a recent shoot.
        When the card is configured for that experience, each becomes a
        natural opening for a more meaningful conversation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Keep the next step relevant and easy. Invite someone to save your
        details, explore your work, or get in touch. You keep your reusable
        card, while they leave with a clear way to reconnect.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <p class="fine">
    NFC functionality varies by device, reader, and record format. Direct-data
    reading can work offline with compatible readers; websites and hosted
    services normally require internet access.
  </p>

